QUESTION NUMBETR 1

Condition that causes deadlock:
P1 locks R1 first.
P2 locks R2 first.
P1 waits for R2, which is held by P2.
P2 waits for R1, which is held by P1.
Both processes wait indefinitely for each other.
This creates a circular wait, resulting in a deadlock.
Optimization to eliminate the deadlock:
Use a fixed resource locking order for all processes.
Make both P1 and P2 lock R1 first, then R2.
This removes the circular wait condition.
Deadlock is prevented without changing the process logic.


QUESTION NUMBER 2

Cause of low CPU utilization:
CPU utilization is only 30% despite ready processes.
Processes frequently switch between Ready → Waiting → Ready.
Processes spend most of their time waiting for I/O operations.
CPU remains idle while processes are blocked.
Frequent state transitions reduce overall CPU efficiency.
System-level optimization:
Use asynchronous I/O or buffering to reduce waiting time.
Improve CPU scheduling to execute other ready processes while one waits for I/O.
Increase multiprogramming so the CPU always has a process to execute.


